STAGE 5 — MAXIMUM-EFFICIENCY BATCHED FORMALIZATION AND CI

5.1 Organize formalization into dependency-aware batches

Divide the remaining work into logically coherent batches.

Prioritize:

1. Foundational definitions.
2. Elementary results.
3. Supporting lemmas.
4. Intermediate propositions.
5. Main theorems.
6. Corollaries and consequences.

Only work on a dependent result when its required foundations are available.

5.2 Follow this exact continuous execution cycle

For EVERY batch, execute the following sequence:

1. Identify the next dependency-ready results.
2. Formalize their complete mathematical proofs.
3. Remove their corresponding "sorry" placeholders.
4. Run local Lean checks.
5. Correct local errors.
6. Submit or execute the appropriate CI/build verification.
7. While CI is running, continue formalizing another independent batch whenever resources permit.
8. Retrieve the previous CI output.
9. Identify and diagnose every reported error.
10. Correct the affected source.
11. Re-run verification.
12. Integrate the verified results.
13. Continue with the next dependency-ready batch.

Do not wait for the entire manuscript to be formalized before beginning verification.

Do not remain idle while CI is running if independent work can proceed.

Do not treat unverified code as completed work.

5.3 Optimize CI usage without overloading it

Use local checks and incremental builds during development.

Run full builds at meaningful integration points.

Avoid:

- Repeated full builds after trivial changes.
- Excessive overlapping CI jobs.
- Unnecessary resource contention.
- Repeated submission of unchanged failing code.
- Abandoning verification to save time.

Reuse reliable incremental build artifacts.

Perform a complete integrated build after all formalization changes have been incorporated.

The objective is the maximum amount of correctly verified mathematical work within the available resources.

5.4 Repair every reported CI failure

For each CI failure:

1. Retrieve the relevant output.
2. Identify the exact failing file and declaration.
3. Identify the underlying cause.
4. Correct the source.
5. Perform local verification.
6. Re-run the appropriate build.
7. Confirm that the correction succeeds.

Do not merely document CI failures and proceed.

Do not mark a batch verified until its verification succeeds.

